{"id":"persona-5s4b.36","title":"attest_peer() builds the same Unattested fallback twice in a nested match","description":"persona-grpc/src/consumer_attest.rs:29-42 — a nested match on two Results, where both failure arms construct the identical value:

    let exe_path = std::fs::read_link(format!("/proc/{}/exe", pid));
    let identity = match exe_path {
        Ok(path) => match hash_file(&path) {
            Ok(hash) => ConsumerIdentity::BinarySha256(hash),
            Err(_) => ConsumerIdentity::Unattested { uid: ucred.uid, pid },
        },
        Err(_) => ConsumerIdentity::Unattested { uid: ucred.uid, pid },
    };

What goes wrong: two levels of nesting and a duplicated literal to express one idea — 'hash the peer binary, else fall back'. The duplicate arms are a maintenance trap: the two Unattested constructions must stay identical, and nothing enforces it, so a later change (adding a field, recording which step failed) will get applied to one arm.

Rewrite — flat, one fallback, no nesting:

    let identity = std::fs::read_link(format!("/proc/{pid}/exe"))
        .ok()
        .and_then(|path| hash_file(&path).ok())
        .map(ConsumerIdentity::BinarySha256)
        .unwrap_or(ConsumerIdentity::Unattested { uid: ucred.uid, pid });

Separately worth noting: both error arms discard the error with `Err(_)`, so a peer that silently downgrades to Unattested leaves no trace of why. A tracing::debug! on the fallback path would make that diagnosable — the whole point of this function is deciding how much to trust the caller.","status":"open","priority":3,"issue_type":"task","owner":"mark@reviewcommit.com","created_at":"2026-09-13T20:58:14.397881047Z","created_by":"agent","updated_at":"2026-09-13T20:58:14.397881047Z","labels":["elegance","review"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"persona-5s4b.36","depends_on_id":"persona-5s4b","type":"parent-child","created_at":"2026-09-13T20:58:14.418386591Z","created_by":"agent","metadata":"{}"}]}

